    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7b577ba0_c205c791",
        "filename": "include/queue.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 143,
      "author": {
        "id": 1287098
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-05-31T21:28:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It sounds like what one wants here is to find a contiguous chunk of queue space so that a copy of multiple elements can be optimized by a memcpy, right?\n\nIf so, is there any reason to pass an \"offset\" and why not instead just say queue_get_contiguous_writable_chunk(queue*, size_t count) which would return - if available - a contiguous chunk of at least \"count\" elements, regardless of offset?\n\nIt feels like a more natural design to me, but maybe I am missing something?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 143,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 143,
        "endChar": 79
      },
      "revId": "6b26280fb45c50b7a731117a549ff44fba09f527",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c1a96c80_4bbc3281",
        "filename": "include/queue.h",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 143,
      "author": {
        "id": 1333748
      },
      "writtenOn": "2019-06-03T15:55:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The memory might not always be continuous. In the example above we have indices 0, 1, 5, 6, 7 available to write. So if you want continuous memory you can either get 0,1 or 5,6,7. The previous implementation of this function always assumed continuous starting at the tail. For the next CL to work we needed to be able to wrap around using the offset.",
